exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

Depth-first walk carrying the path as an array of names. At each node, if it
has hits, emit `path.join(";") + " " + hits`.

That is the whole algorithm. The care goes into what the count means and how
the output is ordered.

## Hint 2

```ts
const walk = (id: number, path: string[]): void => {
  const node = byId.get(id)!;
  path.push(nameOf(node));
  const hits = node.hitCount ?? 0;
  if (hits > 0) add(path.join(";"), hits);
  for (const child of node.children ?? []) walk(child, path);
  path.pop();                 // the line everyone forgets
};
```

`path.pop()` on the way back up is what keeps siblings from inheriting each
other's ancestry. Forget it and every stack after the first is wrong.

## Hint 3

Merging duplicates needs a `Map<string, number>`, and the sort at the end is
what makes the output reproducible — `Array.prototype.sort` on strings is
lexicographic, which is exactly what the flame graph's alphabetical sibling
ordering wants.

The decision left to you: whether to include the root frame in the path.
Renderers accept both; consistency matters more than the choice.

references.solution.ts:
// v3nc05_cpu_profiling / ex4 - reference solution (author only, used by `dojo verify`).

interface Node {
  id: number;
  callFrame: { functionName: string };
  hitCount?: number;
  children?: number[];
}

export function foldStacks(profile: { nodes: Node[] }): string[] {
  const byId = new Map(profile.nodes.map((node) => [node.id, node]));
  const childIds = new Set<number>();
  for (const node of profile.nodes) for (const child of node.children ?? []) childIds.add(child);
  const root = profile.nodes.find((node) => !childIds.has(node.id));
  if (!root) return [];

  const folded = new Map<string, number>();
  const path: string[] = [];

  const walk = (id: number): void => {
    const node = byId.get(id);
    if (!node) return;
    path.push(node.callFrame.functionName === "" ? "(anonymous)" : node.callFrame.functionName);

    const hits = node.hitCount ?? 0;
    if (hits > 0) {
      const stack = path.join(";");
      // Own hits, never the subtree: the renderer sums children itself, and
      // emitting subtree totals makes every box as wide as the root.
      folded.set(stack, (folded.get(stack) ?? 0) + hits);
    }

    for (const child of node.children ?? []) walk(child);
    // Without this, every sibling inherits the previous branch's ancestry.
    path.pop();
  };

  walk(root.id);

  return [...folded.entries()]
    .map(([stack, count]) => `${stack} ${count}`)
    // Lexicographic, which is also how a flame graph orders siblings - so the
    // rendered picture is stable between runs and the file is diffable.
    .sort();
}

